--- Rosetta Code: Variable declaration reset
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Variable_declaration_reset
--- Crow has no declarations: a variable is made by its first assignment and lives
--- in the block that made it. One first assigned in a loop's body is made anew in
--- every round and is gone at the round's end, so no round can see what the round
--- before left in it. The task's loop, with prev first assigned in the body,
---
---     for i in range[len[s]]
---         curr = s[i]
---         if i gt 0 and curr eq prev
---             log[i]
---         prev = curr
---
--- is refused before it runs, in both modes (crow run -i and crow run), rather
--- than run with an empty prev:
---
---     reset.co:4:27: undefined variable 'prev'
---
--- (a while loop is refused alike). So prev is made before the loop, and the
--- body's assignment writes that outer variable: each round reads what the round
--- before it left. Places count from 0.

s = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5]

log['A for loop, prev made before it:']
prev = 0
for i in range[len[s]]
    curr = s[i]
    if i gt 0 and curr eq prev
        log[i]
    prev = curr

log['A while loop, last made before it:']
last = 0
i = 0
while i lt len[s]
    curr = s[i]
    if i gt 0 and curr eq last
        log[i]
    last = curr
    i = i + 1

--- Crow's own way needs no variable carried from round to round: each round
--- compares its item with the one before, and the rounds that find a repeat
--- give their place.
repeats = for i in range[1, len[s]]
    if s[i] eq s[i - 1]
        i
log['Collected without a carried variable: `repeats`']
